{"schema":2,"commit":"241e7dd3541552405e4fcebee5b6733afed370d2","branch":"main","base_sha":"79a96eb7668c8a28fdaac6bb0271e501e3eb2bdc","message":"Record shell-written files in the Attribution working log\n\nA file an agent edits through the shell — `sed -i`, a heredoc, a\nredirect, a generator script — never entered the working log. The Claude\nhook's Bash branch returns at recordClaudeBashWrites, well before the\ncaptureAuthorship call the Write/Edit branch makes, and the codex,\ncopilot and gemini recorders all make on their own paths. Only Claude's\nBash branch was missing it.\n\nThat matters because the working log is what decides line authorship at\ncommit time (invariant I4: the note and the gutter derive from it). The\ndaemon POST this path already did is not a substitute: it carries\nwhole-file ranges, so for a MODIFIED file only the lines whose content\nhash happens to match something already recorded get credited, and the\nlines the agent actually wrote fall through to Human. A newly ADDED file\nlooked fine — the whole file is new content — which is why the gap was\ninvisible in the common case.\n\nSeen on 82fb7bf, whose note credits the AI with the blank comment\nmarkers, the closing brace, and three lines that already existed\nelsewhere in the file, while every line of the comment the agent wrote is\nattributed to the user. Anything that edits through a shell is\nsystematically under-credited, which is most of what a terminal agent\ndoes.\n\nThe capture joins against the work tree that was scanned rather than\npayload.RepoPath, which resolves to the MAIN repo root and would be the\nwrong base inside a linked worktree. RecordEdit diffs against the stored\nbaseline (or HEAD on first sight) and credits only genuinely-changed\nlines, so this does not sweep the user's own work into the AI's column —\nasserted by the second test.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 \u003cnoreply@anthropic.com\u003e","totals":{"added_lines":132,"ai_added_lines":0,"human_added_lines